mod Exchange.EscrowFsmDslReference
  ###
  The real `Std.Fsm` transition-graph DSL, applied to the exact same escrow
  graph as `lib/escrow_fsm.cure`'s hand-written `transition/3`: typed event
  payloads (`LockFunds(amount, quote_id)`, `CounterpartyMatched(trade_id)`),
  a `when` guard, and three `terminal` states. This is the declarative
  surface documented in `docs/FSM_GUIDE.md`; writing it here exercises the
  compiler's compile-time graph verification (reachability, deadlock-freedom,
  duplicate-transition and payload-consistency checks) on the real diagram.

  This declaration lives in its own module, deliberately never `use`d by
  anything else in this project, for two independent reasons:

  1. Its generated `State`/`Event` constructors (`Created`, `FundsReserved`,
     ...) would otherwise collide with `Exchange.EscrowFsm`'s own
     hand-written `EscrowState`/`EscrowEvent` constructors of the same name
     if both lived in one module -- unlike a plain nested `mod`, an `fsm`
     macro's generated constructors are NOT independently namespaced from
     the rest of their enclosing module in this compiler build.
  2. In *project-mode* compilation (`cure compile <dir>`, and therefore
     `cure test`), calling this macro's generated API (`.start`, `.send`,
     even the pure `.decide`) from another module -- same file or not --
     fails with an internal `missing_module` resolution error. Declaring it
     on its own, as done here, is unaffected.

  Validate this declaration with:

    cure run lib/escrow_fsm_dsl_reference.cure

  `cure run` compiles this file -- fully expanding and verifying the `fsm`
  macro -- before calling `main/0` below. `main/0` here does nothing but
  return `:ok`; printing it is a deliberately boring, reliable signal that
  the DSL declaration above it is well-formed and its graph passed
  verification. Try deleting a `terminal` line or an edge below and
  re-running to see the compiler reject the graph (`fsm_deadlocked_state`,
  `fsm_unreachable_state`, ...) before `main/0` ever runs.
  ###

  use Std.Fsm
  use Std.Io

  rec EscrowData
    amount: Int
    quote_id: String
    trade_id: String

  fsm Escrow with EscrowData
    terminal Cancelled
    terminal Completed
    terminal Refunded

    Created --LockFunds(amount: Int, quote_id: String)--> FundsReserved
      when amount > 0
      update EscrowData{data | amount: amount, quote_id: quote_id}
    FundsReserved --QuoteExpired--> Cancelled
    FundsReserved --CounterpartyMatched(trade_id: String)--> ExecutingSwap
      update EscrowData{data | trade_id: trade_id}
    ExecutingSwap --BankConfirmed--> Completed
    ExecutingSwap --SettleTimeout--> Refunding
    ExecutingSwap --BankFailed--> Refunding
    Refunding --RefundCompleted--> Refunded

  ## Nothing here touches `Escrow`'s generated API -- see the header comment
  ## for why not. This only proves the declaration above compiled.
  fn main() -> Atom =
    let _: Unit = Std.Io.println("escrow_fsm_dsl_reference: graph verified")
    :ok
